   220	
   221	The student visits arm/vision states absent from demonstration data. In the
   222	measured v1a run, ten plates reached the rack but the student stayed closed in a
   223	settled pose for over 1,000 steps. Flipping state dimensions 7, 8, 44 and 45 did
   224	not change its gripper prediction. Following the DAgger idea (Ross et al., 2011),
   225	we label observations from student rollouts and train on the union. The teacher
   226	cannot provide reliable arbitrary-state arm targets, so these new episodes
   227	supervise **only gripper action dimension 18**. This experiment does not supply
   228	new grasp-approach or arm corrections.
   229	
   230	`method/dagger/collect.py` runs in the sim environment, calls the unchanged
   231	`advaitic_eval.environment.make_environment()`, and uses the evaluator's
   232	`PolicyProcess`/worker with `submission.policy:make_policy` in the model env.
   233	That factory constructs the exact `BCPolicy` with the requested checkpoint.
   234	Only RGB/state cross the worker boundary. Returned chunks, reset behavior,
   235	thresholding and temporal ensembling use the evaluation path unchanged. Oracle
   236	checks happen at **every pre-action control step**, including inside chunks;
   237	labels belong to that observation, not the resulting next state. Sensors are a
   238	local read-only copy of the teacher diagnostics; no cross-worktree imports,
   239	controller overrides or pose injection are used.
   240	
   241	Training-only oracle constants live in `method/dagger/diagnostics.py`:
   242	
   243	| Label | Conditions at the observation being labelled |
   244	| --- | --- |
   245	| Open, `-1` | Touching the target rack, nearest of its six sites at distance ≤ 0.05 m, tilt ≤ 20° |
   246	| Closed, `+1` | Held by a gripper, plate height ≥ reset height + 0.03 m, no target-rack contact |
   247	| Undefined | Everything else; no action supervision |
   248	
   249	Tilt uses the fixed task convention: angle of the rotated plate local +z to
   250	world -y. Open takes priority. These three open predicates are exactly the
   251	oracle; there are no extra table/floor exclusions. Undefined labels leave the
   252	student action in the array but its mask is False. `label_mask` is bool `(T,20)`;
   253	all dimensions other than 18 are always False. Each summary logs counts and
   254	fractions of open/closed/undefined steps, plus initial/final geometry, maximum
   255	lift, held-step count, first held/placed step, slot and termination.
   256	
   257	Every rollout, including failure and early cutoff, is saved as
   258	`DIR/ep_<seed>.npz`, with the teacher-compatible RGB/stride/state/action/seed/
   259	slot/success keys and the new mask. `action` is the executed student action with
   260	only defined dim-18 labels replaced. `action_executed` records what actually
   261	entered the environment. Default execution is pure student; `--relabel-execute`
   262	instead executes defined gripper labels, letting collection see post-release
   263	states while continuing the student's arm chunks. The boolean mode is saved
   264	in both NPZ and JSONL. Keep modes in separate output directories.
   265	
   266	`--max-steps` defaults to 1,700. `--stop-after-placed N` defaults to 150 and
   267	stops after N additional actions from the first state meeting the open condition.
   268	Both must be positive. Natural environment termination takes precedence. A
   269	cutoff is recorded as a training rollout cutoff; it cannot count as scored
   270	success. Outcome precedence is `success`, `fell` (robot fall or plate touching
   271	floor), `no_grasp` (maximum lift < 3 cm), `placed_not_released` (final open
   272	geometry and still held), `reached_target_then_failed` (open geometry visited
   273	earlier), then `other`. These are broad replay-style diagnostic categories;
   274	raw geometry is retained to make them auditable. A cutoff shortly after
   275	placement can be categorized as reached-target failure even after release.
   276	
   277	NPZ publication is atomic and precedes the shared `summary.jsonl` append.
   278	Completed files embed their summary so resume repairs an interrupted log append.
   279	Per-seed locks prevent duplicate collection and a short JSONL lock serializes
   280	appends across OS processes. Rerunning the same command skips completed files;
   281	an interrupted episode restarts from reset. Checkpoint/source hashes and rollout
   282	settings in `collector_config.json` prevent mixing configurations. Incompatible
   283	files or missing previously logged episodes fail explicitly. The seed parser
   284	uses Python's stop-exclusive `A:B` convention, refuses all seeds below
   285	2,000,000, and conservatively limits collection to `[2000000,3000000)` so dev
   286	and reviewer/public evaluation blocks cannot be collected accidentally.
   287	
